# uG24 cross-check kit

Five programs built by the uG24 LLVM toolchain, each with the console output
and the instruction count our simulator produces. The point is to let a second,
independently written simulator be checked against a second implementation
without either side having to trust the other's paperwork.

Built from commit `@COMMIT@` at `@BUILT@`.

## Run them

```sh
your-simulator 01_hello.elf > got.txt
diff got.txt 01_hello.expected
```

Every `.expected` file is what our simulator prints, and every answer in it is
also checkable by hand — `25 + 17` is 42, the sum of 1 to 100 is 5050, 8! is
40320, 48879 is `0xBEEF`. If a line disagrees, the arithmetic in the program
says which of us is wrong, without either simulator being the referee.

All five `.expected` files are also byte-for-byte identical to the same source
compiled with host `gcc` and run against glibc on x86-64 — including
`05_float`, where `float` arithmetic printed to six decimals rounds the same on
both. So they are not merely "what our simulator did": they agree with a
completely unrelated compiler and C library. `make-handoff.sh` re-checks that
every time it builds the kit.

`*.count` holds the number of instructions our simulator retired. The same
image on a correct simulator retires exactly the same number, so a count that
differs by more than a rounding of how you count `WFI` is a real divergence and
worth chasing even when the output matches.

## If a program runs for ever and prints nothing

Read this first; it is the failure a separately written simulator actually hit.

`UART_STATUS` (`0xFF01`, bit 0 = ready) is **this project's invention**. The uG24
core contains no UART, so neither source document says a status register exists,
and a loader that does not model it reads zero. The old runtime waited for that
bit for ever, so the program spun on the three instructions at the top of its
transmit loop and printed nothing at all — which looks exactly like a
miscompiled program and is not one.

**The runtime in this kit settles the handshake once, on the first byte.** If
nothing answers within a bounded wait, it stops asking and writes the byte
anyway, so output appears whether or not the register is implemented. You do not
need to implement `UART_STATUS`, and you do not need to patch anything. If you do
implement it, return bit 0 set when the transmitter can accept a byte; it then
costs one read per character.

Signature of the old failure, for the record: `PC` parked on a 3-instruction
loop, `R0` holding the first character to be sent (`0x48`, `H`), and `DPTR0`
pointing at `0xFF01`.

## If your core has no multiplier

`no-multiplier/` holds every program again, built with `-mcpu=ug24-base`:
no `MUL` and no `DIV` anywhere, in the program **or** in the runtime library.
Use that directory instead if your core does not implement them, and tell us —
it is a build flag on our side, not something to patch into an image.

The top-level images do use `MUL`, which is in the encoding spreadsheet and is a
real instruction of the part. A core that implements the full instruction set
should run them.

## If you have run an earlier kit: two things changed on 1 October 2026

The hardware team confirmed the ABI and the ELF identity, so **images built
before and after that date are not interchangeable**.

1. **`e_machine` was `0x9240` and is now `0xBA51`.** A simulator that checks it
   will reject or warn about every file in this kit until that constant is
   updated. The relocation names and numbers changed with it: 0 `R_UG2408_NONE`,
   1 `R_UG2408_16`, 2 `R_UG2408_PCREL10`, 3 `R_UG2408_8`, 4 `R_UG2408_LO8`,
   5 `R_UG2408_HI8`, 6 `R_UG2408_32`. A loader of executables never sees these.
2. **The memory map is now split: ROM `0x0000`-`0x7FFF`, RAM `0x8000`-`0xFEFF`.**
   So `.data` has a load address in ROM and a run address in RAM, and `crt0`
   copies it before `main`. **A loader that ignores `p_paddr` stops working
   here**, silently and only for initialised variables. It was getting away with
   it while the map was one flat region. `04_memory` is the program that catches
   it.

The register-level ABI changed too — arguments in `R0`-`R3`, returns in `R0`,
`X0` or `X0:X1`, callee-saved `R6`-`R11` — but none of that is visible to a
simulator. It only matters if you are writing uG24 assembly by hand.

## Read the console address out of the ELF, do not guess it

The uG24 core contains no UART, so neither the ISA specification nor the
encoding spreadsheet says where console output goes — that is a decision of
whichever SoC wraps the core, and it cannot be derived from those documents.

Every image here therefore publishes its own peripheral map as absolute symbols
in the symbol table:

```sh
llvm-readelf -s 02_integers.elf | grep -E '__ug24_|__mmio_'
```

`uG24-platform.md`, included here, has the map, all nine symbol names, a thirty
line loader that reads them, and the rest of what these files assume that the
ISA documents do not cover. Read it before anything else.

## Four things that catch a new simulator

1. **Start at `e_entry`, not at address 0.** It is genuinely not always zero:
   the vector table's `LJA` at 0 works only if you honour the entry point.
2. **Load by `p_paddr` from the program headers**, not by section name.
   `04_memory` is the one that catches this: `.data` has a load address
   distinct from its run address, and `crt0` copies it before `main`.
3. **`WFI` must halt when no interrupt can arrive**, and wait when one still
   can. `crt0` ends in `wfi` / `jr`, so a simulator that always waits hangs at
   the end of every program. A program calling `exit()` writes the exit
   register instead.
4. **Sizes are not the host's.** `int` is 16 bits, `long` 32, `long long` 64,
   pointers 16, and `double` is IEEE **single** precision, 32 bits, like AVR.
   All of those are confirmed by the hardware team except `double`.
   That last one is why `05_float` prints `355 / 113 = 3.141593` and stops
   there: a host printing `3.14159292` is using a 64-bit double and is not
   disagreeing.

## If a program prints nothing

Check the console address first, not the instruction decode. `01_hello` is the
smallest test of that: a handful of stores to the transmit register and the end
of `main`. If it prints nothing, the address is wrong; if it prints and then
hangs, `WFI` is wrong.

`SHA256SUMS` is there so we can confirm we are both looking at the same bytes.
